{ I'm writing this in Autumn 2024.

I made this study in 2018 when I was first learning the London System and while it may be OK for beginners, it is not good for intermediate/advanced level.

IM Alex Banzea used to have a comprehensive London System playlist on youtube but since then he removed many of the videos and has probably put the theory in Chessable courses - large paid ones and small free ones. If you have a chessable account you can get free courses on the london like his and many others. That can teach you more than I can put in a study.
There are lots of free chessable courses and I extract as much from the free courses as I can all the time.
There are no ads or shady stuff because chessable makes money from paid courses.

But there are many more great resources like lichess studies and youtube videos that can give you more nudges in the right direction, ... and the most important tool is the lichess Masters Database where you can see the most common moves by Masters. You should develop your own repertoire yourself. You can learn a lot of ideas without a course/coach just by looking at the masters database and asking questions to the engine. }
1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Bf4 c5 4. e3 Nc6 5. Nbd2 { this is one of the modern move orders } 5... Qb6 6. dxc5! { it gets very sharp from here, you have to look in the database yourself (press E)
if a move is confusing, try to figure out the reason behind it using the engine.
If you look in the masters database you can play several different moves and eventually you will only be following a single game. If it's your own study you can click "insert" and it will paste the entire game for you to quickly go through. You should be viewing and adding as many games as possible so that you can get an overview of what types of plans the grandmasters play, what moves they go for and what they avoid. } 6... Qxb2 7. Rb1 Qc3 8. Bb5 e6 9. O-O { I'm not going to cover this theory anymore, so you have to find from other lichess studies, youtube videos, free chessable courses, and using the masters database so you can develop your repertoire yourself.
Thank you for looking at this study and good luck. } *


